  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nGulf moisture, surging into the region ahead of the remnants of \nHurricane Polo, crashed into a stalled frontal boundary, bisecting \nthe forecast area. This interaction brought a steady rainfall to \nlocations along and south of I-80 last night. By 7 AM, amounts had \nranged from around 0.25\" up to a max of 1\"-2\" from Lincoln to Omaha, \nand 1\"-3\" over the corners of far southeast Nebraska and southwest \nIowa. \n\nWe saw a brief break in precip this morning, before the second wave \ndeveloped with increasing low level convergence as the warm front \nassociated with the land-fallen hurricane lifts north. The 18Z OAX \nsounding PWAT reached 2.08\". This not only beats the daily max of \n1.69, but is also the highest recorded PWAT to date, outside of the \nrange from June 24th-August 23rd. Deep warm cloud layers, around \n12,000 ft, within skinny, well saturated soundings confirm efficient \nheavy rain producing showers and storms can be expected through the \nafternoon and evening hours, as Polo's remnants surge northeast \ntoward Kansas. Some locations receiving repeated rounds of heavy \nrainfall could see flash flooding, especially this evening and \novernight.\n\nWhile cloud cover may stifle potential energy development this \nafternoon, the warm front lifting north may bring just enough \ninstability to produce deeper, potentially severe, convection. A \ndecently strong LLJ will nose into the region this evening, bringing \naround 40kts of deep layer sheer. Spin associated with Polo will \nhelp curve hodographs across southeast Nebraska. This suggests that \nif a strong storm or two are able to develop, damaging winds and a \ntornadoes would be possible this evening. \n\nRain will likely continue through the overnight hours and into early \nThursday, before pushing south and east of the forecast area. When \nall is said and done, widespread rainfall amounts of 2\"-4\" look \nlikely across southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa, with a narrow \nband of locally higher amounts, closer to 5\"-6\"+ possible. \nSignificant rises on rivers in southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa \nlook likely, with a few already forecast to reach minor flood stage. \n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nFor those who are sick and tired of all of this rain, we have some \ngood news for you - cooler and drier weather is on the way! \nTemperatures will dip into the 60s Friday through the weekend, with \ndry northwesterly flow overspreading the region. A ridge building \nover the southwestern CONUS will shift eastward by the middle of \nnext week, bumping temperatures back into the 70s. There's a slight \nchance for a few showers popping up in the northwesterly flow by mid \nto late week, however widespread rain chances look nil for the \nforeseeable future.",
